   "id": "a2-21",
   "name": "Business finance: long-term loans, leasing and short-term loans"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "• A long-term loan (more than one year) from a bank finances big investments; it is recorded in account 162 (loans from credit institutions). The business pays back the capital plus interest (a cost, account 67).\n• Leasing (crédit-bail): the business uses an asset (a vehicle, a machine) owned by a leasing company and pays rent; at the end it may buy the asset for a small price. A leasing contract states the period, the payments and the purchase option.\n• Short-term loans (less than one year): bank overdrafts, short-term credit, supplier credit; they help pay daily expenses.\nInterest = loan × rate × time (simple interest).\nBefore borrowing: compare interest rates, check the business can repay, read the contract.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "L": 2000000,
   "r": 10,
   "t": 3
  },
  "problem": "A business borrows {L} FCFA at {r}% simple interest per year for {t} years. Find the interest.",
  "steps": [
   "Interest = loan × rate × time.",
   "{L} × {r} ÷ {100} × {t} = {L * r * t / 100} FCFA."
  ],
  "answer": "{L * r * t / 100} FCFA",
  "checks": []
 },
